From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:24:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Send voice recordings and videos as such, not as documents

A file is sent to Telegram as what its content is, rather than always as a
document:

- A file pasted, dropped or attached with /paste whose content is Opus in
  Ogg (audio/x-opus+ogg, as shared-mime-info tells it) gets a [Voice#N]
  placeholder and goes as a voice message, or, switched to [name#N], as a
  file. Vorbis and other audio stay files. A voice message never joins an
  album.
- With the account option "send-files-by-content" on (off by default), a
  file sent by file transfer ("Send File...") goes as guessAttachmentKind()
  tells by its content, as a pasted one does: a picture as a photo, a video
  as a video, an Opus recording as a voice message, anything else as a
  document. It is uploaded as the matching file type, and its kind is kept
  with the transfer until the upload is done.

A voice message's length comes from the last Ogg page of its stream, less
the pre-skip in its header, counted at 48 kHz. TDLib wrapped a voice
message's file in inputVoiceNote in 1.8.66, which TDLIB_API_INPUT_VOICE_NOTE
tells.

This is the pull request as it stands after review, squashed; it takes in:
- c708167 "Send a pasted Opus recording as a voice message, by its content" (Mikhail T.)
- 362c4c5 "Send a file by file transfer as what its content is, if the account says so" (Herman van Hazendonk)

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Submitted [https://github.com/UnitedMarsupials/tdlib-purple/pull/30]
